use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Disclosure:
    description:
        Disclosure lets the user show and hide a region of content with a
        trigger, such as a "Show details" button or the heading of a section
        that folds away. It belongs to the component that has both the
        trigger and the region.

    trigger:
        Pressing the trigger switches the region between shown and hidden. It
        is pressed like any button: clicking, tapping, or using the keyboard.
        The trigger tells assistive tools whether the region is shown, and
        which region it controls.

    state:
        The region is either shown or hidden. It can start either way. The
        component using it is told each time this changes, and can also set it
        at any time, such as showing a section that holds an error.

    hiddenContent:
        Hidden content can't be reached with the keyboard or by assistive
        tools. Whether the platform's find-in-page can still search it, and
        show the region when it finds a match, is up to the chosen library.
        Whether hidden content stays rendered is Presence.

    partialCollapse:
        A region can shrink to a set size instead of disappearing, such as
        showing the first few lines of a long description with "Show more".
        The trigger still says whether the region is fully shown. Whether the
        cut-off part can be reached while it is shrunk is up to the chosen
        library.

    direction:
        A region usually opens downwards. It can also open sideways, such as a
        side panel that grows wider.

    focus:
        Showing the region doesn't move focus. It stays on the trigger, so the
        user can press it again to hide the region. If focus is inside the
        region when it is hidden, it goes back to the trigger rather than
        being lost.

    disabled:
        A disabled trigger can't be pressed, and the region stays shown or
        hidden as it was. The component can still change it.

    groups:
        A component can hold several disclosures together, such as a stack of
        sections where opening one closes the others. That component decides
        whether more than one region can be shown at once, and whether the
        last shown region can be hidden. Moving between the triggers with the
        arrow keys, if it allows that, is Traversal.

    nonGoals:
        - The region doesn't float above the page or close when the user
            clicks elsewhere. A panel that does is a Popover, which uses
            Dismissal
        - Hovering doesn't show the region. Only pressing the trigger does
